but my question here is why now why is  this developing now because people have  suffered from rare conditions since the  start of human history why is it taking  off at this point exactly so the reason  why it's taking off at this point is  because we're actually able to sequence  people's genomes for a reasonable price  so when the first human genome was  sequenced in the human genome project it  took them three billion dollars and 13  years to do and so that's just not a  price at which it makes sense now the  cost of sequencing genes is falling and  now it's starting to get towards $1,000  per person and so that means you can  start sequencing large numbers of people  and start to look across large databases  to see if there are trends if there are  patterns that tell you you know what  genes are related to what symptoms are  what conditions and also to look for  outliers people who are extremes and  have really strange mutations what's the  most valuable part of this process is  that the database of people with these  rare genetic mutations yeah so having  large databases is truly valuable to  drug companies now because you can  really search for ties between you know  you know what your genes are doing and  what your what your body how your body  is behaving and how did the medical  community come to realize the potential  power of these mutations such as  excessive resilience to pain or  excessive bone formation to treat  opposite conditions like osteoporosis I  what was the aha moment yeah so normally  in the past when Pharma was trying to  understand what our genes did they'd  have to look in mice so you would like  create a knockout mice say you knock out  a gene a specific gene and Masson you  say oh you know what does that do in the  mouse but mice are not humans they're  they're really different so they're not  always the best proxy but you can't  really do this to humans you can't grab  humans off the street and say I'm gonna  knock out one of your genes and see what  happens to you and so the aha moment  here is really when you find someone  like Stephen Peet the guy I interviewed  who doesn't feel pain he's like one of  the drug makers described them as  nature's experiments and being able to  find people like this tells you a lot  about the human body and how other  normal humans like you and I might  behave if we can mimic these conditions  that's certainly very valuable but  what's in it for Steven peat does he get  paid to offer his science up for his  body up for science says is the drug  company looking for a cure for him so  that is one of the ironic realizations I  had when I was working on this story is  that they're people like Steven peat who  are extremely rare there may be a few  dozen people in the world who don't feel  pain  but it really doesn't make sense for  drug makers to be making drugs for this  population just because it's you know  there's not a really a big market for  them so talking to Steven he he said to  me he's willing to take part in research  studies because he wants to further our  understanding of pain as a whole and he  does realize that there is a need for  good new painkillers for the you know  for the masses like you and me but for  now nobody's working on anything to help  him all right bloom rats Carol and Trent  thank you so much fascinating article  once again in this week's edition of  Bloomberg Businessweek carolyn chen  joining us from san francisco
 

